LinkedIn News Asia shared a post: Labour markets will be reshaped for longe=
r lives

The pursuit of living longer will have far-reaching consequences on the way=
 we work. As longevity science accelerates and more people expect to remain=
 active well into their 70s and 80s, employers and governments will redesig=
n work around a population that simply isn=E2=80=99t aging the way it used =
to.

Firms will be pushed to rehire older employees and create programs to suppo=
rt senior employment. Air-conditioning maker Daikin Industries, Ltd. offers=
 a preview: in Japan, the company now rehires employees up to age 70 with p=
erformance-based bonuses. French tech firm Atos and insurer AXA UK are movi=
ng in the same direction, reskilling and retaining workers over 50 to help =
them stay in the workforce.=20

Countries will also adapt. We could see retirement ages rise, governments e=
xpand grants for flexible work and lifelong retraining, and more retirees a=
llowed to work tax-free without losing benefits. Such changes are taking sh=
ape in nations with aging populations like Japan, Germany and France =E2=80=
=93 and are set to spread more widely.

AI-driven restructuring may reshape the labour market in the short term, bu=
t the demographic reality remains unavoidable as fertility rates hit record=
 lows and populations age rapidly, with one in six people projected to be o=
ver 60 by 2030.

"When different generations work together, it becomes a powerful source of =
strength for the organisation," says Daikin President Naofumi TAKENAKA. Lea=
ders will increasingly view longer lives as an economic opportunity =E2=80=
=94 and will redesign work to match a future where multidecade careers beco=
me the norm.

